   ----------\n747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n748 |         Constant input matrix.\n749 | \n750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n752 | \n753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n755 | \n756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n764 | \n765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n766 |         Number of iterations.\n767 | \n768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n770 | \n771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n773 | \n774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n775 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n776 | \n777 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n778 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n779 | \n780 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n781 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n782 | \n783 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n784 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n785 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n786 | \n787 |     verbose : int, default=0\n788 |         The verbosity level.\n789 | \n790 |     Returns\n791 |     -------\n792 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n793 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n794 | \n795 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n796 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n797 | \n798 |     n_iter : int\n799 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n800 | \n801 |     References\n802 |     ----------\n803 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n804 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n805 |     \"\"\"\n806 |     start_time = time.time()\n807 | \n808 |     beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss)\n809 | \n810 |     # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n811 |     if beta_loss < 1:\n812 |         gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - beta_loss)\n813 |     elif beta_loss > 2:\n814 |         gamma = 1.0 / (beta_loss - 1.0)\n815 |     else:\n816 |         gamma = 1.0\n817 | \n818 |     # used for the convergence criterion\n819 |     error_at_init = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n820 |     previous_error = error_at_init\n821 | \n822 |     H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n823 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n824 |         # update W\n825 |         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n826 |         delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n827 |             X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n828 |         )\n829 |         W *= delta_W\n830 | \n831 |         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n832 |         if beta_loss < 1:\n833 |             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n834 | \n835 |         # update H\n836 |         if update_H:\n837 |             delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n838 |                 X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n839 |             )\n840 |             H *= delta_H\n841 | \n842 |             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n843 |             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n844 | \n845 |             # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n846 |             if beta_loss <= 1:\n847 |                 H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n848 | \n849 |         # test convergence criterion every 10 iterations\n850 |         if tol > 0 and n_iter % 10 == 0:\n851 |             error = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n852 | \n853 |             if verbose:\n854 |                 iter_time = time.time()\n855 |                 print(\n856 |                     \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds, error: %f\"\n857 |                     % (n_iter, iter_time - start_time, error)\n858 |                 )\n859 | \n860 |             if (previous_error - error) / error_at_init < tol:\n861 |                 break\n862 |             previous_error = error\n863 | \n864 |     # do not print if we have already printed in the convergence test\n865 |     if verbose and (tol == 0 or n_iter % 10 != 0):\n866 |         end_time = time.time()\n867 |         print(\n868 |             \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds.\" % (n_iter, end_time - start_time)\n869 |         )\n870 | \n871 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R language (R-project):\n    #     - \"mixOmics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show th