\n109 | def __getattr__(name):\n110 |     if name == \"load_boston\":\n111 |         msg = textwrap.dedent(\n112 |             \"\"\"\n113 |             `load_boston` has been removed from scikit-learn since version 1.2.\n114 | \n115 |             The Boston housing prices dataset has an ethical problem: as\n116 |             investigated in [1], the authors of this dataset engineered a\n117 |             non-invertible variable \"B\" assuming that racial self-segregation had a\n118 |             positive impact on house prices [2]. Furthermore the goal of the\n119 |             research that led to the creation of this dataset was to study the\n120 |             impact of air quality but it did not give adequate demonstration of the\n121 |             validity of this assumption.\n122 | \n123 |             The scikit-learn maintainers therefore strongly discourage the use of\n124 |             this dataset unless the purpose of the code is to study and educate\n125 |             about ethical issues in data science and machine learning.\n126 | \n127 |             In this special case, you can fetch the dataset from the original\n128 |             source::\n129 | \n130 |                 import pandas as pd\n131 |                 import numpy as np\n132 | \n133 |                 data_url = \"http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/datasets/boston\"\n134 |                 raw_df = pd.read_csv(data_url, sep=\"\\\\s+\", skiprows=22, header=None)\n135 |                 data = np.hstack([raw_df.values[::2, :], raw_df.values[1::2, :2]])\n136 |                 target = raw_df.values[1::2, 2]\n137 | \n138 |             Alternative datasets include the California housing dataset and the\n139 |             Ames housing dataset. You can load the datasets as follows::\n140 | \n141 |                 from sklearn.datasets import fetch_california_housing\n142 |                 housing = fetch_california_housing()\n143 | \n144 |             for the California housing dataset and::\n145 | \n146 |   